1
00:00:00,974 --> 00:00:03,844
Speaker 2: Here is where most love
stories from old Hollywood would end,

2
00:00:04,224 --> 00:00:08,934
with a breakup, a few gossip columns,
and two people quietly moving on with

3
00:00:08,934 --> 00:00:14,986
their lives This one kept going for
another thirty years in two completely

4
00:00:14,986 --> 00:00:19,535
different directions, told by two people
who had lived through the exact same

5
00:00:19,536 --> 00:00:24,196
five years and somehow walked away
remembering two different relationships.

6
00:00:24,926 --> 00:00:28,165
Reynolds never let go of the story
he had built about those years.

7
00:00:28,875 --> 00:00:33,575
In interview after interview, well into
his seventies, he called Field the love

8
00:00:33,575 --> 00:00:38,335
of his life, unprompted, long after
either of them had married other people

9
00:00:38,336 --> 00:00:40,546
and moved on in every visible way.

10
00:00:41,385 --> 00:00:44,646
He told one reporter that he
did four movies with her, spent

11
00:00:44,646 --> 00:00:47,865
five years with her, and that
she was the one who got away.

12
00:00:48,995 --> 00:00:52,716
He called ending the relationship one of
the biggest disappointments of his life.

13
00:00:53,516 --> 00:00:57,795
In his memoir, he devoted pages to
their romance, to the chemistry that

14
00:00:57,795 --> 00:01:02,306
first sparked on a film set, and to
a regret that apparently never faded,

15
00:01:02,315 --> 00:01:03,985
no matter how much time passed.

16
00:01:04,935 --> 00:01:09,045
When journalists brought his comments
back to Field, her answers were careful

17
00:01:09,045 --> 00:01:11,705
at first and grew more direct over time.

18
00:01:12,435 --> 00:01:16,836
She said she was flattered in the
beginning that a man once so famous

19
00:01:16,855 --> 00:01:18,985
kept naming her as his greatest love.

20
00:01:19,836 --> 00:01:25,955
She also said Reynolds was, in her words,
"a complicated man," a phrase she used

21
00:01:25,965 --> 00:01:29,836
often enough that it started to sound
like a warning rather than a compliment.

22
00:01:30,736 --> 00:01:33,865
Later, promoting her own
memoir, she went further still.

23
00:01:34,675 --> 00:01:37,695
She said Reynolds had reimagined
the relationship in his own

24
00:01:37,705 --> 00:01:41,575
retelling, that she had not been
as important to him in real time as

25
00:01:41,575 --> 00:01:43,575
he later claimed once she was gone.

26
00:01:44,385 --> 00:01:49,555
She suggested, gently but pointedly,
that he simply wanted, in hindsight,

27
00:01:49,916 --> 00:01:53,686
to have had the thing he never fully
had while they were actually together.

28
00:01:54,615 --> 00:02:00,425
Then, in twenty-eighteen, Field published
her own memoir called In Pieces.

29
00:02:01,245 --> 00:02:04,326
It told a very different story
than the one Reynolds had spent

30
00:02:04,336 --> 00:02:05,905
decades repeating in public.

31
00:02:06,785 --> 00:02:10,935
Field wrote that their relationship
was often controlling and emotionally

32
00:02:10,935 --> 00:02:14,195
difficult in ways she had not
fully understood or been able to

33
00:02:14,196 --> 00:02:15,925
name at the time it was happening.

34
00:02:16,705 --> 00:02:20,906
She connected it to her own childhood,
describing years of sexual abuse

35
00:02:20,926 --> 00:02:25,955
by her stepfather, the actor and
stuntman Jock Mahoney, and suggested

36
00:02:25,965 --> 00:02:29,485
that part of what drew her toward
Reynolds may have been an old familiar

37
00:02:29,486 --> 00:02:33,875
pattern rooted in that childhood she
was still quietly trying to survive.

38
00:02:34,805 --> 00:02:38,215
She also wrote about his struggles
with drugs and his declining health,

39
00:02:38,665 --> 00:02:42,835
details Reynolds himself had spent years
keeping carefully out of public view.

40
00:02:43,645 --> 00:02:49,535
Perhaps the single most striking detail
in the whole story is this: Field revealed

41
00:02:49,535 --> 00:02:53,476
that she had not spoken to Reynolds for
roughly the last thirty years of his life.

42
00:02:54,475 --> 00:02:58,255
Not a phone call, not a
reunion, nothing at all.

43
00:02:59,236 --> 00:03:03,995
All while he continued publicly and
repeatedly to describe her as the love

44
00:03:03,995 --> 00:03:06,166
of his life to anyone who would listen.

45
00:03:07,256 --> 00:03:12,216
Reynolds died in 2018, the same
year Field's memoir was released.

46
00:03:13,126 --> 00:03:17,276
Asked about his death, she said he
would remain in her history and in

47
00:03:17,276 --> 00:03:19,195
her heart for as long as she lived.

48
00:03:19,946 --> 00:03:23,886
A careful, generous sentence from a
woman who had just spent an entire book

49
00:03:23,896 --> 00:03:25,766
explaining why she needed to leave him.

50
00:03:26,726 --> 00:03:31,066
She also said plainly that she
was glad he would never read what

51
00:03:31,066 --> 00:03:34,515
she had written about their years
together because it would've hurt him.

52
00:03:35,595 --> 00:03:37,915
So which version is true?

53
00:03:38,355 --> 00:03:41,445
The love story he never stopped
telling for the rest of his life,

54
00:03:42,115 --> 00:03:45,675
or the difficult, complicated
years she finally described fully

55
00:03:45,676 --> 00:03:47,935
on her own terms decades later?

56
00:03:48,926 --> 00:03:53,146
The honest answer is that both
were true at the same time for

57
00:03:53,146 --> 00:03:54,736
the two people who lived them.

58
00:03:55,746 --> 00:04:00,145
Reynolds spent the second half of his life
mourning a version of their romance frozen

59
00:04:00,145 --> 00:04:06,106
at its very best moment, the four days
on a film set that felt like four years.

60
00:04:06,955 --> 00:04:12,295
Field spent those same decades trying
to explain, mostly to herself, why

61
00:04:12,305 --> 00:04:16,595
she had needed to leave a relationship
that looked from the outside like the

62
00:04:16,595 --> 00:04:19,055
greatest love story Hollywood ever filmed.

63
00:04:19,945 --> 00:04:21,866
He called her the one who got away.

64
00:04:22,716 --> 00:04:25,366
She called it something closer
to a pattern she finally

65
00:04:25,366 --> 00:04:26,895
recognized and escaped.

66
00:04:27,915 --> 00:04:32,415
Maybe the real ending is that Hollywood's
most famous unrequited love story was

67
00:04:32,426 --> 00:04:37,295
never actually about two people missing
each other across 30 years of silence.

68
00:04:38,036 --> 00:04:41,286
It was about one person who never
stopped writing the same script in

69
00:04:41,286 --> 00:04:45,685
his head, revising the same five years
until they matched the ending he wished

70
00:04:45,685 --> 00:04:50,445
he had, and one person who had already
closed the book, put it on a shelf,

71
00:04:50,725 --> 00:04:54,176
and built an entirely different life
around the space where he used to be.

72
00:04:54,935 --> 00:04:59,885
Two people, one relationship, and
30 years of silence in between.

73
00:05:00,816 --> 00:05:03,426
Reynolds spent his telling
the world what he lost.

74
00:05:04,066 --> 00:05:08,335
Field spent hers finally telling the
world what it actually cost her to leave.

75
00:05:09,445 --> 00:05:13,685
Only one of them ever got to hear the
other's version, and by the time hers

76
00:05:13,685 --> 00:05:16,515
reached the world, he was already gone.
